(2) THE MECHANISM, RE-DERIVED FROM SOURCE -- PASS
=====================================================================
I did not take the worker's citation. Note first that the version question is
moot: the go-sdk copy and the pinned toolchain-module copy of the file are
byte-identical --
  $ md5sum {toolchain@v0.0.1-go1.26.6,go-sdk/go}/src/cmd/go/internal/test/test.go
    d893044ee5a2a5dbdc60cf7a77a6fc8d   (both)

computeTestInputsID(), $(go env GOROOT)/src/cmd/go/internal/test/test.go:
  2032  case "getenv":
  2033      fmt.Fprintf(h, "env %s %x\n", name, hashGetenv(name))     <- NO InDir guard
  2037  case "stat":
  2041      if a.Package.Root == "" || search.InDir(name, a.Package.Root) == "" { break }
  2046  case "open":
  2050      if a.Package.Root == "" || search.InDir(name, a.Package.Root) == "" { break }
  2068  func hashGetenv(name string) { ...; h.Write([]byte(v)) }      <- hashes the VALUE
So the getenv case is unguarded while stat/open are gated on the package Root --
exactly the asymmetry the comment asserts.

The chain from this test to that opcode, all three links read on this machine:
  $GOROOT/src/os/exec/lp_unix.go:60   path := os.Getenv("PATH")
  $GOROOT/src/os/env.go:101-104       func Getenv(key string) { testlog.Getenv(key); ... }
  $GOROOT/src/internal/testlog/log.go:50  func Getenv(name) { log.Getenv(name) }

And the direct observation, which settles it without any reading: I built the
package test binary and ran it with -test.testlogfile to dump the exact input
list `go test` hashes.
  $ go test -c -o <scratch>/acct.test ./internal/handlers/accounts/
  $ <scratch>/acct.test -test.run TestSyncWarnings_ClientRegressionHarness \
      -test.testlogfile=<scratch>/testlog.txt        (rc 0)
  $ sort -u <scratch>/testlog.txt
      getenv GOTMPDIR
      getenv PATH                                              <- the tracked PATH read
      getenv TMPDIR
      open /dev/null
      open <REPO>/web/templates/pages/accounts.html            <- inside module root
      open /tmp
      open <scratch>/nodedir/node
      open <scratch>/extracted_sync_warnings.js
      stat <REPO>/internal/handlers/accounts/testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js
      stat <scratch>/nodedir/node
Exactly one `getenv PATH` line, produced by this test's exec.LookPath("node")
(warnings_client_regression_test.go:150). accounts.html and the harness JS both
appear, both under the module root, hence both past the InDir gate.

Sentence-by-sentence audit of the new comment paragraph (lines 41-57):
  a. "`go test` records the PATH environment string as part of a package's cache
     key" -- TRUE. test.go:2032-2033 + hashGetenv hashing the value; `getenv
     PATH` in the testlog.
  b. "this test's own call to exec.LookPath("node") reads PATH via os.Getenv,
     and that read is what gets tracked" -- TRUE. The three-link chain above,
     plus the single getenv PATH testlog line.
  c. "installing or removing node, which is what normally changes PATH, busts
     the cache and forces a real re-run" -- TRUE as measured (E4 below): with
     node's bin dir removed from PATH the package re-ran (0.203s, not "(cached)")
     and failed. "normally" is a hedge, and it is the same hedge my own
     attempt-6 verdict prescribed ("which is how node normally disappears"); the
     package-manager case where PATH does not change is not left to the hedge --
     it is named explicitly in the very next sentence ("a base image losing the
     node package but not the PATH string it set").
  d. "editing accounts.html or the harness JS busts it too, since `go test` also
     tracks file reads within the module root and both files live there" -- the
     claimed effect is TRUE and measured (E5 below); both files are inside the
     boundary (testlog above). PRECISION NOTE, non-blocking: accounts.html is an
     `open` (os.ReadFile at line 105) but the harness JS is a `stat` (os.Stat at
     line 177), hashed by hashStat over size/mode/mtime, not a read. "tracks file
     reads" is a compression of open+stat rather than a false statement about
     behaviour, and the behaviour it predicts is exactly what I observe.
  e. "But PATH the string, not node's presence, is the tracked input" -- TRUE;
     E2 vs E3 below are the two halves of that.
  f. "if node disappears while PATH stays byte-for-byte identical ... `go test`
     may replay a cached pass and this guard will not fire" -- TRUE, E2. "may"
     is correctly hedged (the entry can have been trimmed or never saved).
  g. "`go clean -testcache` or `-count=1` settles it" -- TRUE, both measured.

