=====================================================================
(4) NEITHER OVERSTATED NOR UNDERSTATED -- PASS
=====================================================================
My attempt-6 verdict's remedy was: "Delete [the parenthetical] from the covered
list. If a cache sentence is wanted at all, the checkable one is: a warm test
cache is invalidated when the PATH string changes -- which is how node normally
disappears -- and when accounts.html or the harness JS is edited, because both
sit under the module root; a cached green result is NOT invalidated by node being
removed at an unchanged PATH."
What shipped is that, component for component, plus a remedy sentence:
  - deleted: `grep -n 'even a warm test cache\|execs node\|outside its package
    directory\|cache never replays' <test file> README.md <harness JS>` -> rc 1,
    no matches anywhere in the tree.
  - PATH-string invalidation .............. present (clauses a-c)
  - accounts.html / harness JS edits ...... present (clause d)
  - the NOT-invalidated exception ......... present (clause f)
  - remedy ................................ added (clause g), an improvement
Placement: not buried. The paragraph opens "One case deserves stating precisely
rather than folding into that list", sits immediately after the seven-invocation
list, and is the only place the list is qualified -- so a reader cannot take the
list as unconditional and miss this. Not overstated either: it is scoped by "one
narrow case", by "may replay", and by naming the two concrete situations that
produce it; nothing in it suggests the guard is unreliable in general, and the
two sentences before it restate what does bust the cache. The README wording is
the same shape ("That guarantee is for a fresh run", "only survives ... if PATH
also stays byte-for-byte unchanged", "in the common case").

